🔹 Program 1: Function Overloading

📌 Description

Function Overloading is an example of compile-time polymorphism in C++.

It allows multiple functions to have the same name but different parameter lists.

In this program, the "add()" function is overloaded to perform addition with:

- 🔢 Two integers
- 🔢 Two double values
- 🔢 Three integers

The compiler determines which "add()" function should be called based on the number and data types of the arguments.

🎯 Concept Used

Compile-Time Polymorphism — Function Overloading

💡 Key Point

«Same function name + different parameters = Function Overloading»
